## 3. Exactness over `Q`

All rank computations use

```text
P = 2^127 - 1.
```

The Lucas-Lehmer test for exponent 127 returns zero, so `P` is prime.

For complete-block relation ranks, the established universal bound is

```text
max squared row norm = 12,
rank <= 15,
P^2 > 12^15.
```

Across the full functional-lift execution, the observed global bounds were

```text
max functional-row squared norm = 45,
max functional width            = 25,
max relation-row squared norm   = 24,
max relation-row count          = 25.
```

The certificate checks

```text
P^2 > 45^25,
P^2 > 24^15.
```

Hence every modular rank used in the finite obstruction agrees with the rational rank: a nonzero rational minor cannot vanish modulo `P` because its determinant magnitude is strictly below `P` by Hadamard.

For the closure inequality itself, subtracting `rank7 = |C_7|-5` is conservative even without using equality for the enlarged canonical relation family: the complete-block relation rows alone have exact rank `rank7`, so the actual functional extra rank is at most the reported `rank(total)-rank7`. The full execution bounds this quantity by ten everywhere.

## 4. Consequence

A realization of the remaining `C:(5,12,12), d_6=5` branch would require seven-row singleton-functional extra rank eleven. The exhaustive exact scan finds maximum extra rank ten. Therefore

```text
C:(5,12,12), d_6=5 is impossible.
```

The `d_6=3` and `d_6=4` sides of this profile were already closed. Therefore

```text
C:(5,12,12) is impossible.
```

The companion final profile `C:(4,11,10)` was already closed. Thus the current eight-no-root `k=7` finite split has no surviving profile.

This is a finite-layer closure only. It does not alter the canonical declaration that Target C-II and Erdős Problem #99 remain open.
